1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,307
Here is a translation page
running on your laptop.

2
00:00:03,627 --> 00:00:07,418
Type good afternoon everyone,
choose French, press Translate,

3
00:00:07,418 --> 00:00:09,344
and it answers: Bonjour à tous.

4
00:00:09,583 --> 00:00:11,567
Now send the address to a classmate.

5
00:00:11,887 --> 00:00:15,289
On their laptop,
localhost 8086 finds nothing,

6
00:00:15,289 --> 00:00:20,613
because localhost always means this machine,
and nothing runs on theirs.

7
00:00:20,850 --> 00:00:24,221
Lecture 1 gave one fix:
rent a virtual machine.

8
00:00:24,541 --> 00:00:28,577
The page gets a public address,
but the machine is billed all night,

9
00:00:28,577 --> 00:00:30,535
while nobody translates anything.

10
00:00:30,783 --> 00:00:32,532
So here is today's question.

11
00:00:32,852 --> 00:00:37,909
Can this page have a public URL,
and cost nothing while nobody is using it?

12
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:40,091
That is the promise of serverless.

13
00:00:40,411 --> 00:00:43,259
A copy of your app appears
when a request arrives

14
00:00:43,259 --> 00:00:45,467
and disappears when the traffic stops.

15
00:00:45,787 --> 00:00:49,137
By the end,
the same image also runs as a job.

16
00:00:49,457 --> 00:00:52,101
It takes a passage from a private bucket,

17
00:00:52,101 --> 00:00:55,131
speaks it in French,
saves its files and exits.

18
00:00:57,167 --> 00:00:59,710
To follow along, have your clone, Docker,

19
00:00:59,710 --> 00:01:03,183
the gcloud tool
and your own project with billing ready.

20
00:01:03,417 --> 00:01:06,251
A tracker in the corner
names the README step,

21
00:01:06,251 --> 00:01:09,454
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

22
00:01:09,774 --> 00:01:12,073
Follow the console steps first;

23
00:01:12,073 --> 00:01:15,854
the gcloud boxes are alternatives,
not extra steps.

24
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:19,441
First, where this lecture sits:

25
00:01:19,441 --> 00:01:22,667
inside the prototype phase
of the course project,

26
00:01:22,667 --> 00:01:24,227
where deployment begins.

27
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:26,942
Of the Cheese App's four parts,
three are done.

28
00:01:27,262 --> 00:01:28,845
Model deploy is left,

29
00:01:28,845 --> 00:01:32,238
and milestone 3 asks you
to deploy your model

30
00:01:32,238 --> 00:01:35,283
or API with Cloud Functions or Cloud Run.

31
00:01:35,517 --> 00:01:38,971
The lecture's serverless:
code runs only when needed,

32
00:01:38,971 --> 00:01:40,359
no servers to set up,

33
00:01:40,359 --> 00:01:43,794
and costly hardware only
for the step that needs it.

34
00:01:44,033 --> 00:01:47,639
Last week's Vertex AI
training job was one case;

35
00:01:47,639 --> 00:01:52,673
today, functions and Cloud Run;
next week, endpoints and pipelines.

36
00:01:52,917 --> 00:01:55,712
Its map is one app, four ways to deploy it.

37
00:01:56,032 --> 00:01:59,771
Across, what you hand over,
a code file or a container;

38
00:01:59,771 --> 00:02:03,306
down, where it runs,
a rented machine or serverless.

39
00:02:03,626 --> 00:02:07,509
Lectures 1 and 2 filled the top row;
today fills the bottom.

40
00:02:07,750 --> 00:02:10,459
The README's four parts sit
in that bottom row.

41
00:02:10,779 --> 00:02:12,678
Part 1 hands over a file.

42
00:02:12,998 --> 00:02:18,075
Parts 2 to 4 hand over one image,
first as a web service, then as a job.

43
00:02:18,317 --> 00:02:19,960
And notice what we deploy.

44
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:23,928
Not the translation model,
which an outside service runs,

45
00:02:23,928 --> 00:02:26,168
but the application code around it.

46
00:02:26,488 --> 00:02:28,877
Your Cheese App backend will play that role.

47
00:02:29,117 --> 00:02:30,333
Two small notes.

48
00:02:30,653 --> 00:02:34,854
The course page links to sections
the rewritten README no longer has,

49
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:36,072
so start at the top.

50
00:02:36,392 --> 00:02:39,293
And we follow the README
the course link serves now,

51
00:02:39,293 --> 00:02:41,469
not the older commit in the course map.

52
00:02:42,817 --> 00:02:43,990
Before you begin.

53
00:02:44,310 --> 00:02:48,031
Select your course project
and check its project ID,

54
00:02:48,031 --> 00:02:49,892
not just its display name;

55
00:02:49,892 --> 00:02:52,012
the command boxes need the ID.

56
00:02:52,332 --> 00:02:56,527
Confirm billing is on and that
you may create these resources;

57
00:02:56,527 --> 00:02:58,625
if not, ask course staff first.

58
00:02:58,867 --> 00:03:01,768
Choose a region: the README uses Toronto.

59
00:03:02,088 --> 00:03:07,166
The speech model later uses us-central1,
a separate setting for part 3.

60
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:12,521
The worksheet names what you will create:
the function, the web service, the job,

61
00:03:12,521 --> 00:03:16,126
two runtime accounts,
and two buckets you name in part 4.

62
00:03:16,367 --> 00:03:18,714
Step 4: clone the repository.

63
00:03:19,034 --> 00:03:23,077
The README says clone or download,
so our git clone is extra,

64
00:03:23,077 --> 00:03:26,458
and so is the git log
showing the commit we follow.

65
00:03:26,778 --> 00:03:28,036
Pause and clone it.

66
00:03:28,284 --> 00:03:32,841
An extra listing shows older files
at the root, including main.py

67
00:03:32,841 --> 00:03:36,118
and a Dockerfile,
which the README never uses;

68
00:03:36,118 --> 00:03:37,329
don't build them.

69
00:03:37,649 --> 00:03:42,726
Today's work sits under tutorial:
function for part 1, app for the image.

70
00:03:42,984 --> 00:03:44,660
Then the terminal setup,

71
00:03:44,660 --> 00:03:48,222
starting with gcloud auth login,
a browser sign-in;

72
00:03:48,222 --> 00:03:51,048
an extra auth list shows no accounts yet.

73
00:03:51,368 --> 00:03:56,701
It logs in the gcloud tool, not the app;
the app's identity comes in part 3.

74
00:03:56,950 --> 00:04:00,973
Seven exports follow:
project, region, image, two names

75
00:04:00,973 --> 00:04:04,630
and two account emails built
from your project ID.

76
00:04:04,950 --> 00:04:07,212
Pause and run them with your real ID.

77
00:04:08,650 --> 00:04:10,013
About this recording:

78
00:04:10,013 --> 00:04:13,258
this machine has no Google account
and no project.

79
00:04:13,578 --> 00:04:17,192
So every gcloud box runs
until its first honest error,

80
00:04:17,192 --> 00:04:18,616
the console is drawn,

81
00:04:18,616 --> 00:04:22,346
and cloud results come
from the README's own recording.

82
00:04:22,883 --> 00:04:24,483
Part 1: a function.

83
00:04:24,803 --> 00:04:29,057
The lecture calls it lightweight code
that an event starts,

84
00:04:29,057 --> 00:04:33,742
like a file upload, a database change,
or today, an HTTP request.

85
00:04:34,062 --> 00:04:36,792
It is billed while it runs
and scales by itself.

86
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:39,783
Its four steps: open the console,

87
00:04:39,783 --> 00:04:43,167
enable the APIs,
write a Python file, deploy.

88
00:04:43,487 --> 00:04:47,986
One name has changed:
Cloud Functions are now Cloud Run functions,

89
00:04:47,986 --> 00:04:52,212
so the README starts
at Cloud Run, Services, Write a function.

90
00:04:52,450 --> 00:04:54,135
That opens Create service.

91
00:04:54,455 --> 00:04:56,546
Enable the APIs if it asks.

92
00:04:56,866 --> 00:05:00,364
Name the service translate-function
and choose your region.

93
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:05,848
For the runtime, pick Python 3.12;
the older README said 3.10.

94
00:05:06,168 --> 00:05:08,004
Choose Allow public access,

95
00:05:08,004 --> 00:05:11,266
so anyone with the URL may call it,
then Create.

96
00:05:11,516 --> 00:05:14,439
Open the Source editor;
pause and create yours.

97
00:05:14,759 --> 00:05:18,476
A gold tag:
the function bills as a Cloud Run service,

98
00:05:18,476 --> 00:05:20,540
and every deploy runs a build.

99
00:05:20,783 --> 00:05:24,004
Replace main.py with the tutorial's handler.

100
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:29,764
The decorator marks
an HTTP function called hello_http.

101
00:05:30,084 --> 00:05:34,970
It takes text, source and destination
from the request, each with a default.

102
00:05:35,216 --> 00:05:39,077
Beside it, the lecture's sketch
passes source second, by position.

103
00:05:39,397 --> 00:05:42,752
In this library the second slot
is the destination,

104
00:05:42,752 --> 00:05:47,355
so source and destination would swap;
the handler passes them by name.

105
00:05:47,600 --> 00:05:50,843
Replace the requirements file
with two exact pins.

106
00:05:51,163 --> 00:05:55,557
The newer googletrans is asynchronous
and would break this simple handler.

107
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:59,747
Set the Function entry point to hello_http.

108
00:06:00,067 --> 00:06:01,397
In the README's words,

109
00:06:01,397 --> 00:06:04,540
it is the Python function
the framework should call,

110
00:06:04,540 --> 00:06:06,595
not the file name or service name.

111
00:06:06,915 --> 00:06:10,237
Select Save and redeploy
and wait for the revision,

112
00:06:10,237 --> 00:06:13,216
a deployed version
of your code and settings,

113
00:06:13,216 --> 00:06:14,275
to become ready.

114
00:06:14,595 --> 00:06:15,768
Pause and do it.

115
00:06:16,016 --> 00:06:19,813
So the slide's step,
create a Python file, is the short version.

116
00:06:20,133 --> 00:06:25,254
The console wants the file, its dependencies
and the name of the function to call.

117
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:28,029
What runs your function?

118
00:06:28,349 --> 00:06:31,559
The lecture says you manage
no servers or containers,

119
00:06:31,559 --> 00:06:33,981
and that is true: you don't manage them.

120
00:06:34,301 --> 00:06:36,520
But Google builds a container anyway.

121
00:06:36,767 --> 00:06:39,199
Save sends your three inputs to Cloud Build.

122
00:06:39,519 --> 00:06:42,866
It installs the requirements once,
at build time,

123
00:06:42,866 --> 00:06:48,330
configures the Functions Framework,
and publishes an image to Artifact Registry.

124
00:06:48,650 --> 00:06:51,359
Cloud Run starts a revision from that image.

125
00:06:51,600 --> 00:06:57,033
In each instance, the Functions Framework
runs a small Flask app under gunicorn,

126
00:06:57,033 --> 00:07:00,496
on every interface,
at the port Cloud Run provides.

127
00:07:00,816 --> 00:07:05,637
On each request, it looks up your function
by the entry point name and calls it.

128
00:07:05,883 --> 00:07:09,019
So the lecture's file cell
has a container inside.

129
00:07:09,339 --> 00:07:12,262
That is why there is an image
to clean up at the end.

130
00:07:12,500 --> 00:07:16,745
This layer also runs on your laptop,
as an extra local stand-in.

131
00:07:17,065 --> 00:07:20,540
The same two pins install
the Functions Framework,

132
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:24,084
Flask, gunicorn and googletrans,
as the build does.

133
00:07:24,333 --> 00:07:29,645
Start it with target hello_http,
and it prints nothing while it serves.

134
00:07:29,965 --> 00:07:33,571
That silence is normal:
the framework logs only errors.

135
00:07:33,817 --> 00:07:37,764
You paste main.py
but type translate as the entry point.

136
00:07:38,084 --> 00:07:39,385
Does the build fail?

137
00:07:39,705 --> 00:07:42,265
If not, what fails,
and where would you see it?

138
00:07:51,100 --> 00:07:53,980
Installing the requirements works;
they are fine.

139
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:55,857
But the deployment fails.

140
00:07:56,177 --> 00:07:59,420
When the new revision starts,
the framework looks for the name.

141
00:07:59,740 --> 00:08:03,772
The file main.py should contain
a function named translate.

142
00:08:04,092 --> 00:08:06,609
But it found hello_http.

143
00:08:06,850 --> 00:08:10,306
On Cloud Run, you find this in the logs,
not in your code.

144
00:08:10,626 --> 00:08:12,994
That is the first of three places to look.

145
00:08:14,033 --> 00:08:17,926
Step 1: in service details,
copy the URL near the top;

146
00:08:17,926 --> 00:08:20,881
the build page is not your app's address.

147
00:08:21,116 --> 00:08:23,943
Step 2: open it with no arguments;

148
00:08:23,943 --> 00:08:28,326
the README's recording shows the default
in Spanish:

149
00:08:28,326 --> 00:08:29,927
Bienvenido a AC215.

150
00:08:30,247 --> 00:08:31,422
Our local stand-in,

151
00:08:31,422 --> 00:08:34,514
the same handler under the same framework,
agrees.

152
00:08:34,983 --> 00:08:37,244
Step 3: append the query string.

153
00:08:37,564 --> 00:08:39,605
The question mark begins it,

154
00:08:39,605 --> 00:08:43,687
ampersands split the pairs,
and %20 is an encoded space.

155
00:08:44,007 --> 00:08:48,103
Each pair fills one line of the handler,
and its default steps aside.

156
00:08:48,350 --> 00:08:51,870
The recording shows Bonjour à tous,
and so does the stand-in.

157
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:57,587
Step 4: try Greek, Hindi,
or zh-cn for simplified Chinese.

158
00:08:57,907 --> 00:08:59,358
Pause and try a few.

159
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:04,510
The checkpoint: one endpoint,
different inputs, no redeploy;

160
00:09:04,510 --> 00:09:08,208
and source and destination are languages,
not regions.

161
00:09:08,483 --> 00:09:11,235
Step 5: open Observability, Logs.

162
00:09:11,555 --> 00:09:15,118
The recording found its French
request with status 200.

163
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:19,245
While we recorded,
the provider timed out once:

164
00:09:19,245 --> 00:09:21,150
the client got status 500,

165
00:09:21,150 --> 00:09:26,211
and the server log showed the timeout,
raised from line 12 of main.py.

166
00:09:26,531 --> 00:09:28,386
A retry soon after worked.

167
00:09:28,633 --> 00:09:29,816
The README warns:

168
00:09:29,816 --> 00:09:33,992
the googletrans library wraps
an unofficial outside service,

169
00:09:33,992 --> 00:09:36,740
so check the logs, not a finished build.

170
00:09:36,983 --> 00:09:38,903
Now the gcloud alternative.

171
00:09:39,223 --> 00:09:42,986
In a new project
it may first ask to enable APIs

172
00:09:42,986 --> 00:09:45,730
or create a repository; answer yes.

173
00:09:46,050 --> 00:09:50,339
With the placeholder,
the deploy stops at the project ID check;

174
00:09:50,339 --> 00:09:53,879
with a real-format ID,
it asks you to sign in first.

175
00:09:54,283 --> 00:09:56,304
The next line stores the URL;

176
00:09:56,304 --> 00:09:59,717
the curl lines ran unchanged
against the stand-in

177
00:09:59,717 --> 00:10:01,643
and answered Bonjour à tous.

178
00:10:02,516 --> 00:10:04,812
From the README's More detail box:

179
00:10:04,812 --> 00:10:09,876
a cold start starts an instance of your app,
not necessarily a new machine.

180
00:10:10,196 --> 00:10:12,372
No fixed idle time predicts it.

181
00:10:13,417 --> 00:10:14,417
Part 2.

182
00:10:14,654 --> 00:10:18,857
Now imagine a containerized web app
already working on your laptop.

183
00:10:19,177 --> 00:10:23,060
Sharing localhost shares nothing,
so we deploy that same image.

184
00:10:23,300 --> 00:10:27,805
Step 1, with Docker running:
docker run, remove when done,

185
00:10:27,805 --> 00:10:32,388
publish port 8086 to 8080,
and the tutorial's public image.

186
00:10:32,708 --> 00:10:33,860
Pause and run it.

187
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:38,324
The digest equals the README's:
same bytes, same digest.

188
00:10:38,644 --> 00:10:43,033
The image is built for amd64,
which Cloud Run needs;

189
00:10:43,033 --> 00:10:46,495
on Apple Silicon it runs under emulation.

190
00:10:46,734 --> 00:10:52,601
Then gunicorn listens on 0.0.0.0, port 8080.

191
00:10:52,921 --> 00:10:54,829
That is every network interface,

192
00:10:54,829 --> 00:10:57,870
so connections
from outside the container reach it.

193
00:10:58,117 --> 00:11:00,592
How did Python turn into gunicorn?

194
00:11:00,912 --> 00:11:03,704
First, the image has two start-up slots:

195
00:11:03,704 --> 00:11:07,824
an empty entrypoint, and a command,
translation_demo serve.

196
00:11:08,144 --> 00:11:11,323
Docker runs the entrypoint,
then the command,

197
00:11:11,323 --> 00:11:16,058
and arguments you pass replace the command;
part 4 depends on this.

198
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:22,431
That command takes its port from the PORT
variable and replaces itself with gunicorn:

199
00:11:22,431 --> 00:11:23,874
1 worker, 4 threads.

200
00:11:24,117 --> 00:11:25,504
Extra checks agree.

201
00:11:25,824 --> 00:11:31,370
docker top shows only gunicorn,
a master and a worker; no Python is left.

202
00:11:31,690 --> 00:11:36,970
docker ps shows the door,
8086 outside and 8080 inside,

203
00:11:36,970 --> 00:11:40,138
so 8080 on the laptop is refused.

204
00:11:40,384 --> 00:11:43,349
Step 2: open localhost 8086.

205
00:11:43,669 --> 00:11:47,118
An extra curl shows
status 200 from gunicorn,

206
00:11:47,118 --> 00:11:50,645
the title,
and the disabled Play audio button.

207
00:11:50,884 --> 00:11:52,441
Step 3: translate.

208
00:11:52,761 --> 00:11:57,330
The button sends JSON in a post
request and gets back Bonjour à tous,

209
00:11:57,330 --> 00:11:59,780
once the browser decodes that escape.

210
00:12:00,100 --> 00:12:01,294
Pause and try it.

211
00:12:01,534 --> 00:12:04,760
The query string from part 1 gets 405 here,

212
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:08,211
method not allowed:
this app wants JSON posts.

213
00:12:08,531 --> 00:12:10,686
A made-up language gets 400.

214
00:12:10,934 --> 00:12:15,590
Step 4: Play audio is disabled,
and its request gets 503:

215
00:12:15,590 --> 00:12:17,142
speech is disabled.

216
00:12:17,462 --> 00:12:18,766
Stop with Ctrl+C;

217
00:12:18,766 --> 00:12:22,987
gunicorn is the main process,
so it shuts down cleanly.

218
00:12:23,234 --> 00:12:28,633
One extra experiment:
set PORT to 9090 but keep the pipe at 8080,

219
00:12:28,633 --> 00:12:33,452
and the connection resets;
the log shows gunicorn on 9090.

220
00:12:33,867 --> 00:12:36,662
Pipe to 9090 instead, and it answers.

221
00:12:36,982 --> 00:12:39,840
The right-hand number
must be where the app listens.

222
00:12:44,433 --> 00:12:47,499
The lecture's Cloud Run
runs a whole container,

223
00:12:47,499 --> 00:12:48,934
as a service or a job.

224
00:12:49,254 --> 00:12:53,948
Its steps: open Cloud Run,
enable the APIs, deploy an image.

225
00:12:54,183 --> 00:12:57,244
Open Cloud Run, Services, Deploy container,

226
00:12:57,244 --> 00:13:01,799
and choose Deploy one revision
from an existing container image.

227
00:13:02,119 --> 00:13:04,941
Paste the same image,
name it translation-demo,

228
00:13:04,941 --> 00:13:06,322
and choose your region.

229
00:13:07,416 --> 00:13:10,787
Select Allow public access
and Request-based billing.

230
00:13:11,107 --> 00:13:14,883
Under scaling,
minimum 0 instances and maximum 2.

231
00:13:15,133 --> 00:13:17,446
In the container settings:

232
00:13:17,446 --> 00:13:22,250
port 8080, 512 MiB, 1 CPU,
and the command left alone;

233
00:13:22,250 --> 00:13:25,501
Cloud Run sets PORT and knocks on it.

234
00:13:25,821 --> 00:13:30,941
Then a 120 second timeout
and 4 concurrent requests per instance.

235
00:13:31,183 --> 00:13:32,183
Why 4?

236
00:13:32,442 --> 00:13:37,924
Each instance runs gunicorn with 4 threads,
so it never gets more than it can serve.

237
00:13:38,166 --> 00:13:41,079
With request-based billing
and a minimum of 0,

238
00:13:41,079 --> 00:13:44,054
the meter moves only
while requests are served.

239
00:13:44,374 --> 00:13:48,001
That is the lecture's promise:
pay only when your code runs.

240
00:13:48,321 --> 00:13:52,737
A minimum of 1, or instance-based billing,
bills idle time too.

241
00:13:53,433 --> 00:13:56,270
Concurrency 4, maximum 2 instances.

242
00:13:56,590 --> 00:13:58,724
Ten people press Translate at once.

243
00:13:59,044 --> 00:14:02,500
How many instances start,
and what happens to the last two?

244
00:14:11,350 --> 00:14:14,081
Two instances, the ceiling, with 8 slots.

245
00:14:14,401 --> 00:14:17,750
The other two wait for a free slot
and can be refused.

246
00:14:18,070 --> 00:14:19,220
Raise the maximum,

247
00:14:19,220 --> 00:14:22,221
or raise concurrency together
with the threads;

248
00:14:22,221 --> 00:14:24,107
a higher minimum doesn't help.

249
00:14:24,350 --> 00:14:29,446
Select Create; the recording shows
the public page answering Bonjour à tous,

250
00:14:29,446 --> 00:14:30,921
speech still disabled.

251
00:14:31,241 --> 00:14:33,523
Then check Revision History and Logs.

252
00:14:33,843 --> 00:14:36,677
A revision
is a deployed version of the image

253
00:14:36,677 --> 00:14:37,747
and its settings.

254
00:14:38,067 --> 00:14:39,603
Pause and deploy yours.

255
00:14:39,850 --> 00:14:41,514
Nothing was built this time.

256
00:14:44,050 --> 00:14:46,714
The console took a tag, a movable label;

257
00:14:46,714 --> 00:14:50,109
the revision keeps the digest,
a fixed fingerprint.

258
00:14:50,429 --> 00:14:53,799
To reproduce a deployment later,
deploy by digest.

259
00:14:54,033 --> 00:14:58,065
The gcloud alternative sets the same values
in one command.

260
00:15:01,033 --> 00:15:04,724
First half done:
your laptop's page at a public URL.

261
00:15:05,044 --> 00:15:06,951
But Play audio still waits:

262
00:15:06,951 --> 00:15:11,188
to speak, the app must call an API that asks
who is calling.

263
00:15:11,508 --> 00:15:12,468
Who is it?

264
00:15:13,517 --> 00:15:14,517
Part 3.

265
00:15:14,797 --> 00:15:16,940
The README separates two questions:

266
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:20,920
who may call our public app,
and what the running app may access.

267
00:15:21,167 --> 00:15:25,211
The first door,
who may call it, is Allow public access;

268
00:15:25,211 --> 00:15:28,100
gcloud spells it --no-invoker-iam-check.

269
00:15:28,420 --> 00:15:33,156
If an organization policy forbids it,
the URL answers 403.

270
00:15:34,100 --> 00:15:35,444
So, a quick test.

271
00:15:35,764 --> 00:15:37,940
The service allows public access.

272
00:15:38,260 --> 00:15:40,415
Does that let it call Agent Platform?

273
00:15:47,250 --> 00:15:50,151
No. That door only admits callers.

274
00:15:50,471 --> 00:15:55,741
Calling out goes through the other door,
as whatever identity the service runs as;

275
00:15:55,741 --> 00:16:00,690
until you attach one, that is normally
the project's default compute account.

276
00:16:00,934 --> 00:16:05,554
Step 1: in the API Library,
enable the Agent Platform API,

277
00:16:05,554 --> 00:16:11,131
the console's current name
for what earlier lectures called Vertex AI.

278
00:16:11,367 --> 00:16:16,654
Steps 2 and 3: create a service account
named Translation demo runtime,

279
00:16:16,654 --> 00:16:19,410
with the ID translation-demo-runtime.

280
00:16:19,767 --> 00:16:21,303
Step 4: two roles.

281
00:16:21,623 --> 00:16:24,567
Agent Platform User lets it call the model.

282
00:16:24,887 --> 00:16:29,118
Service Usage Consumer lets it charge
calls to a quota project:

283
00:16:29,118 --> 00:16:32,610
the project whose quota
and bill they count against.

284
00:16:32,930 --> 00:16:34,807
The app names your project there.

285
00:16:35,127 --> 00:16:38,327
Leave both principal fields
empty and select Done.

286
00:16:38,567 --> 00:16:40,786
Step 5: note the account's email.

287
00:16:41,106 --> 00:16:43,365
No key to create or download:

288
00:16:43,365 --> 00:16:47,163
in lecture 3 a JSON key sat
in a secrets folder;

289
00:16:47,163 --> 00:16:48,508
here none exists.

290
00:16:48,828 --> 00:16:53,948
The recording shows the account enabled,
with no keys; pause and create yours.

291
00:16:54,184 --> 00:17:00,259
The gcloud alternative enables the API,
creates the account, and adds the two roles;

292
00:17:00,259 --> 00:17:03,080
both bindings are on the whole project.

293
00:17:04,117 --> 00:17:07,059
Attaching the account
takes a permission of yours:

294
00:17:07,059 --> 00:17:09,237
Service Account User on that account.

295
00:17:09,557 --> 00:17:13,376
That is your deployment right,
separate from the account's own roles.

296
00:17:13,617 --> 00:17:16,092
Step 1: open translation-demo.

297
00:17:16,412 --> 00:17:18,211
Step 2: on the Security tab,

298
00:17:18,211 --> 00:17:21,361
choose your runtime account,
and check its email.

299
00:17:22,684 --> 00:17:27,103
A small gap: the README promises this access
in section 3.3,

300
00:17:27,103 --> 00:17:30,492
but that section grants
Token Creator instead.

301
00:17:30,812 --> 00:17:35,718
Project owners already have it;
in a restricted project, ask course staff.

302
00:17:35,967 --> 00:17:38,506
Step 3: three environment variables.

303
00:17:38,826 --> 00:17:43,398
ENABLE_SPEECH, true;
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT, your project ID;

304
00:17:43,398 --> 00:17:46,420
and GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION, us-central1.

305
00:17:46,740 --> 00:17:50,600
The service runs in Toronto,
the model in us-central1:

306
00:17:50,600 --> 00:17:52,244
two different settings.

307
00:17:52,484 --> 00:17:54,560
Step 4: View diff & redeploy,

308
00:17:54,560 --> 00:17:58,927
keep Serve this revision immediately on,
then Deploy changes.

309
00:17:59,247 --> 00:18:03,748
That makes a new revision;
the old instance is replaced, not edited.

310
00:18:04,068 --> 00:18:06,094
Step 5: wait for the rollout.

311
00:18:06,367 --> 00:18:07,839
Here is the speech path.

312
00:18:08,159 --> 00:18:11,538
The app checks the flag,
the size and the language,

313
00:18:11,538 --> 00:18:16,906
then asks Google's auth library for a token;
on Cloud Run, the badge supplies it.

314
00:18:17,226 --> 00:18:20,255
That lookup
is Application Default Credentials.

315
00:18:20,575 --> 00:18:25,716
Gemini speech in us-central1 sends
audio straight back; no bucket.

316
00:18:26,534 --> 00:18:30,587
Step 6: reload the page,
because it checks the flag when it loads.

317
00:18:30,907 --> 00:18:35,409
Then Translate and Play audio;
the recording shows French audio playing.

318
00:18:35,729 --> 00:18:39,120
A gold tag:
each Play audio is a paid model call.

319
00:18:39,367 --> 00:18:44,700
Step 7: if it fails, check the message
and the logs before changing permissions.

320
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:49,415
Without the flag, you get our captured 503:
speech is disabled.

321
00:18:49,735 --> 00:18:53,639
A missing identity
or a denied call each has its own message.

322
00:18:53,884 --> 00:18:56,999
That is limited control and debugging,
made workable.

323
00:18:57,319 --> 00:19:01,286
The gcloud alternative makes the same change
in one command.

324
00:19:04,217 --> 00:19:06,960
Section 3.3 is optional, and needs uv:

325
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:12,302
how would you develop locally,
without publishing an image for every edit?

326
00:19:12,622 --> 00:19:18,010
Application Default Credentials lets
the same code find a token on Cloud Run

327
00:19:18,010 --> 00:19:19,854
and a file on your laptop.

328
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:23,148
To test the runtime
account's real permissions,

329
00:19:23,148 --> 00:19:24,964
your laptop impersonates it.

330
00:19:25,284 --> 00:19:30,044
You sign in as yourself and receive
short-lived tokens as that account;

331
00:19:30,044 --> 00:19:32,324
no private key is ever downloaded.

332
00:19:32,567 --> 00:19:34,747
Step 1: on the runtime account,

333
00:19:34,747 --> 00:19:38,263
grant your own user
Service Account Token Creator.

334
00:19:38,583 --> 00:19:42,850
Replace any preselected role,
and don't leave Service Account Admin.

335
00:19:43,170 --> 00:19:47,223
Enable the IAM Service Account
Credentials API too.

336
00:19:47,467 --> 00:19:52,025
Step 2 begins with three commands
that repeat step 1 from the terminal;

337
00:19:52,025 --> 00:19:54,272
if you used the console, skip them.

338
00:19:54,517 --> 00:19:57,764
Then two new ones:
an application-default login,

339
00:19:57,764 --> 00:20:01,621
impersonating the runtime account,
and its quota project.

340
00:20:01,941 --> 00:20:04,494
It is separate from gcloud auth login,

341
00:20:04,494 --> 00:20:08,661
and its file stays on your machine,
never in Git or the image.

342
00:20:08,981 --> 00:20:10,274
Pause and sign in;

343
00:20:10,274 --> 00:20:15,018
a restricted project may also need
Service Usage Consumer for you.

344
00:20:15,267 --> 00:20:20,472
Step 3: change into the tutorial app folder,
and run uv sync --frozen.

345
00:20:20,792 --> 00:20:25,379
It installs 41 packages exactly
as the lock file says.

346
00:20:25,699 --> 00:20:29,368
An extra uv tree shows
the six direct dependencies.

347
00:20:29,617 --> 00:20:35,133
Step 4: in the terminal with your exports,
start the app with the three variables

348
00:20:35,133 --> 00:20:38,470
and PORT set to 8086;
this time you set the port.

349
00:20:38,790 --> 00:20:43,654
Pause, run it, then translate
and Play audio at localhost 8086;

350
00:20:43,654 --> 00:20:45,894
with your sign-in, it speaks.

351
00:20:46,134 --> 00:20:49,057
Extra checks:
the button now says Play audio,

352
00:20:49,057 --> 00:20:50,486
and translation works.

353
00:20:50,806 --> 00:20:55,051
Speech answers 503:
Google credentials are unavailable.

354
00:20:55,371 --> 00:20:58,934
That is the missing sign-in,
what you see if you skip step 2.

355
00:20:59,584 --> 00:21:03,146
Step 5: go back up two levels
to the repository root.

356
00:21:04,483 --> 00:21:05,483
Part 4.

357
00:21:05,763 --> 00:21:08,256
A web request has a person waiting,

358
00:21:08,256 --> 00:21:12,030
but a translated audio edition
should take its input,

359
00:21:12,030 --> 00:21:15,448
translate it,
speak it, save the files and exit.

360
00:21:15,768 --> 00:21:16,899
That is a job.

361
00:21:17,133 --> 00:21:21,080
The lecture says a container runs
as a service or as a job.

362
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:25,609
Same image, two programs:
serve keeps listening, with no end;

363
00:21:25,609 --> 00:21:27,885
batch walks four steps and exits.

364
00:21:29,466 --> 00:21:31,837
You have seen this shape before:

365
00:21:31,837 --> 00:21:35,913
last week's Vertex AI training job
was finite work too:

366
00:21:35,913 --> 00:21:37,744
start, train, save, exit.

367
00:21:37,983 --> 00:21:41,994
A job has no URL,
and its disk vanishes when it exits.

368
00:21:42,314 --> 00:21:45,836
So input comes
from Storage and results go back there;

369
00:21:45,836 --> 00:21:49,226
a remote container
cannot see a file on your laptop.

370
00:21:49,466 --> 00:21:52,239
Step 1: Cloud Storage, Buckets, Create.

371
00:21:52,559 --> 00:21:54,857
Step 2: a globally unique name,

372
00:21:54,857 --> 00:21:58,341
such as your project ID
plus translation input.

373
00:21:58,661 --> 00:22:03,393
Step 3: region Toronto, class Standard,
public access prevention,

374
00:22:03,393 --> 00:22:07,834
uniform access, default soft delete,
then Create and Confirm.

375
00:22:08,154 --> 00:22:09,520
Pause and create it.

376
00:22:09,766 --> 00:22:12,731
Step 4: repeat for a separate output bucket.

377
00:22:13,051 --> 00:22:16,012
A gold tag: storage bills while files exist,

378
00:22:16,012 --> 00:22:19,174
and soft delete keeps
deleted ones for a while.

379
00:22:19,416 --> 00:22:24,211
Step 5: create a folder named
run-001 in the input bucket

380
00:22:24,211 --> 00:22:28,248
and upload the sample passage,
four short lines.

381
00:22:28,568 --> 00:22:31,107
Step 6: copy its Storage URI.

382
00:22:31,427 --> 00:22:33,902
Pause, upload and copy the URI.

383
00:22:34,150 --> 00:22:39,121
That run-001 is a prefix you chose,
not a real directory.

384
00:22:39,441 --> 00:22:44,774
The gcloud alternative makes both buckets
and uploads the passage in one block.

385
00:22:48,283 --> 00:22:51,931
Section 4.2: the job gets its own identity.

386
00:22:52,251 --> 00:22:54,188
Create translation-audio-job,

387
00:22:54,188 --> 00:22:57,862
give it the same two project roles,
and note its email.

388
00:22:58,100 --> 00:23:01,859
Then, per bucket:
Storage Object Viewer on the input,

389
00:23:01,859 --> 00:23:04,767
and Storage Object Creator on the output,

390
00:23:04,767 --> 00:23:08,383
which adds files but cannot list
or overwrite them.

391
00:23:08,703 --> 00:23:10,430
The web account needs neither.

392
00:23:10,666 --> 00:23:15,807
Your own identity needs separate rights
to upload, execute and see results.

393
00:23:16,127 --> 00:23:18,752
Pause and create the account
and both grants.

394
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:23,266
The gcloud alternative creates the account
and adds all four grants.

395
00:23:26,133 --> 00:23:32,170
Section 4.3: attaching this account again
takes your Service Account User on it.

396
00:23:32,490 --> 00:23:36,800
In Cloud Run, Jobs, Deploy container
opens Create job.

397
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:40,554
The same image,
the name translation-audio, 1 task.

398
00:23:40,816 --> 00:23:44,123
Container command:
python, then Enter to make a chip.

399
00:23:44,443 --> 00:23:48,198
It fills the entrypoint slot,
which this image left empty.

400
00:23:48,433 --> 00:23:51,957
Container arguments:
eleven values, each its own chip,

401
00:23:51,957 --> 00:23:55,089
and together they take
the command slot's place.

402
00:23:55,409 --> 00:24:00,166
-m, translation_demo, batch,
input URI and your passage,

403
00:24:00,166 --> 00:24:04,753
source en,
destination fr, output URI and your prefix.

404
00:24:05,073 --> 00:24:07,740
Each chip is one item of the argument list.

405
00:24:07,983 --> 00:24:12,085
Then the same three variables,
a task timeout of 5 minutes,

406
00:24:12,085 --> 00:24:15,492
and 0 retries,
because a retry repeats paid work.

407
00:24:15,812 --> 00:24:19,140
On Security,
choose the job's account by its email.

408
00:24:19,460 --> 00:24:24,708
Leave Execute job immediately unchecked,
then select Create; pause and build it.

409
00:24:24,950 --> 00:24:26,907
Paste all eleven into one chip,

410
00:24:26,907 --> 00:24:31,137
and Python looks for a module
with that whole sentence as its name.

411
00:24:31,457 --> 00:24:33,355
The name is cut at the first dot.

412
00:24:35,166 --> 00:24:36,638
Now the other mistake.

413
00:24:36,958 --> 00:24:41,459
You fill all eleven argument chips
but leave Container command empty.

414
00:24:41,779 --> 00:24:43,636
What does the container try to run?

415
00:24:52,483 --> 00:24:55,443
The arguments replace the default command,

416
00:24:55,443 --> 00:24:58,755
and with no entrypoint,
-m becomes the program.

417
00:24:59,075 --> 00:25:02,339
Docker's version fails:
executable not found.

418
00:25:03,483 --> 00:25:04,832
With nothing changed,

419
00:25:04,832 --> 00:25:10,160
the job starts the web server and waits
until the timeout fails it after 5 minutes.

420
00:25:10,400 --> 00:25:14,156
The gcloud alternative
passes the same eleven values,

421
00:25:14,156 --> 00:25:16,778
comma separated, with command python.

422
00:25:18,200 --> 00:25:22,403
Section 4.4:
open the job and select Execute.

423
00:25:22,723 --> 00:25:26,371
The recording shows it succeeding,
with a green check in History.

424
00:25:26,917 --> 00:25:28,432
What would a red mark mean?

425
00:25:28,752 --> 00:25:33,199
Our extra laptop run
without the speech flag exits with status 1,

426
00:25:33,199 --> 00:25:36,346
an honest failure,
so History tells the truth.

427
00:25:37,300 --> 00:25:40,863
Step 2: open the execution,
then Tasks, View logs.

428
00:25:41,183 --> 00:25:43,999
The job exits; it has no web URL.

429
00:25:44,319 --> 00:25:49,515
Its last output is a JSON summary
with the output and manifest locations,

430
00:25:49,515 --> 00:25:51,721
status complete, and the files.

431
00:25:51,967 --> 00:25:56,127
Step 3:
open your output bucket, then run-001.

432
00:25:56,447 --> 00:25:58,964
Inside is a folder with a random name.

433
00:25:59,284 --> 00:26:01,366
Step 4: three files:

434
00:26:01,366 --> 00:26:06,153
translated.txt, speech.wav,
and manifest.json.

435
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:07,552
The order matters.

436
00:26:07,872 --> 00:26:10,851
Each upload is create-only,
and the manifest,

437
00:26:10,851 --> 00:26:13,035
saying complete, is written last.

438
00:26:13,355 --> 00:26:14,549
It is the receipt.

439
00:26:14,783 --> 00:26:16,575
So, the third place to look.

440
00:26:16,895 --> 00:26:19,878
A green check says
the process exited with 0;

441
00:26:19,878 --> 00:26:22,463
the manifest says the work is complete.

442
00:26:22,783 --> 00:26:25,279
Files without a manifest prove nothing.

443
00:26:25,517 --> 00:26:28,824
Execute the same job again,
with the same arguments.

444
00:26:29,144 --> 00:26:31,042
Is the first result overwritten?

445
00:26:37,883 --> 00:26:38,857
No.

446
00:26:38,907 --> 00:26:44,390
A new attempt folder lands beside the first,
and create-only uploads cannot overwrite.

447
00:26:44,710 --> 00:26:46,687
But it is not exactly once:

448
00:26:46,687 --> 00:26:50,641
a retry repeats paid work,
which is why retries are 0.

449
00:26:50,883 --> 00:26:54,680
Step 5: download the text
and the audio with the icons.

450
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:56,536
Pause and run your job.

451
00:26:56,856 --> 00:27:01,742
A gold tag: each execution bills
its running time plus one speech call.

452
00:27:01,983 --> 00:27:04,849
The gcloud alternative executes with --wait,

453
00:27:04,849 --> 00:27:08,170
makes a results folder,
and copies the output down.

454
00:27:08,490 --> 00:27:10,901
The --wait flag
does not stream your app's logs.

455
00:27:11,150 --> 00:27:12,665
The README's checkpoint:

456
00:27:12,665 --> 00:27:17,209
the task finished, the process stopped,
and its files remain in Storage.

457
00:27:18,250 --> 00:27:21,471
Section 4.5: new work from the terminal.

458
00:27:21,791 --> 00:27:23,751
First, a permission of yours:

459
00:27:23,751 --> 00:27:27,063
running a job with overrides
needs its own right,

460
00:27:27,063 --> 00:27:29,599
separate from the job account's roles.

461
00:27:29,833 --> 00:27:31,540
Console only so far?

462
00:27:31,860 --> 00:27:33,694
Run the terminal setup first.

463
00:27:34,014 --> 00:27:37,705
Save a short passage,
under 5,000 characters,

464
00:27:37,705 --> 00:27:42,462
as passage.txt in the repository root,
or copy the sample.

465
00:27:42,782 --> 00:27:46,902
Export both bucket names
and upload it under run-002;

466
00:27:46,902 --> 00:27:49,545
it stops at the placeholder again.

467
00:27:49,783 --> 00:27:53,090
Step 3:
execute the existing job with overrides.

468
00:27:53,410 --> 00:27:57,314
They replace the whole argument list
for this execution only:

469
00:27:57,314 --> 00:27:58,786
a new path and Spanish.

470
00:27:59,106 --> 00:28:01,023
The saved job still says French,

471
00:28:01,023 --> 00:28:03,778
and a flag you leave out
is not taken from it.

472
00:28:05,050 --> 00:28:07,215
Step 4: after it succeeds,

473
00:28:07,215 --> 00:28:11,045
make the results
folder and copy run-002 down.

474
00:28:11,365 --> 00:28:15,098
The README's recording shows
a successful Spanish execution.

475
00:28:15,333 --> 00:28:18,832
Section 4.6: the helper combines all three.

476
00:28:19,152 --> 00:28:21,370
An extra dry run shows its plan.

477
00:28:21,690 --> 00:28:26,267
It uploads to a fresh run folder named
by the time and a random part,

478
00:28:26,267 --> 00:28:29,584
executes with the eleven arguments,
and downloads.

479
00:28:29,833 --> 00:28:33,751
Run for real, it prints each gcloud
command before running it

480
00:28:33,751 --> 00:28:35,742
and stops at the first failure.

481
00:28:36,062 --> 00:28:37,983
Pause and run it with your names.

482
00:28:39,950 --> 00:28:43,790
Step 2:
open the result folder and play speech.wav.

483
00:28:44,110 --> 00:28:49,159
The helper prints Downloaded results
only after it finds a complete manifest:

484
00:28:49,159 --> 00:28:50,339
the receipt again.

485
00:28:53,583 --> 00:28:54,583
Clean up.

486
00:28:54,799 --> 00:28:57,188
Step 1: download what you want to keep.

487
00:28:57,508 --> 00:29:02,159
Step 2: delete translate-function
and translation-demo, then the job.

488
00:29:02,516 --> 00:29:04,281
Step 3: delete your buckets

489
00:29:04,281 --> 00:29:07,679
and the two runtime accounts
once nothing uses them.

490
00:29:07,999 --> 00:29:11,345
Step 4:
the function's image in Artifact Registry,

491
00:29:11,345 --> 00:29:13,887
still there after the service is gone.

492
00:29:14,207 --> 00:29:16,020
Pause and delete them all now.

493
00:29:16,266 --> 00:29:17,354
Two additions.

494
00:29:17,674 --> 00:29:20,617
Soft-deleted files stay
billable for a while;

495
00:29:20,617 --> 00:29:23,690
the build also leaves
source archives and logs.

496
00:29:24,010 --> 00:29:27,786
And, per the README,
keep the shared public image; others use it.

497
00:29:29,233 --> 00:29:30,364
Stuck on a step?

498
00:29:30,684 --> 00:29:34,630
The README ends with a troubleshooting table
of symptoms and checks.

499
00:29:37,566 --> 00:29:38,889
Here is the whole map.

500
00:29:39,209 --> 00:29:42,942
Part 1 handed over a file,
and Cloud Build made the image.

501
00:29:43,262 --> 00:29:45,781
Parts 2 to 4 handed over one image:

502
00:29:45,781 --> 00:29:50,601
a service that scales to zero,
and a job that exits with a receipt.

503
00:29:50,850 --> 00:29:53,943
Now the lecture's three downsides,
and our answers.

504
00:29:54,263 --> 00:29:58,423
Cold starts: a minimum of 1 keeps
an instance warm, for money.

505
00:29:58,666 --> 00:30:02,145
Limited control and debugging:
three places to look,

506
00:30:02,145 --> 00:30:06,560
the build logs, the service logs,
and the job's logs and manifest.

507
00:30:06,800 --> 00:30:10,403
Vendor lock-in:
the image and the Functions Framework ran

508
00:30:10,403 --> 00:30:15,269
on your laptop; only the console,
the permissions and the build are Google's.

509
00:30:15,516 --> 00:30:19,539
Next lecture:
a Vertex AI endpoint for a trained model,

510
00:30:19,539 --> 00:30:20,636
then pipelines.

511
00:30:20,883 --> 00:30:22,824
Test yourself, pausing on each.

512
00:30:23,144 --> 00:30:25,982
One:
place parts 1 and 2 on the lecture's map.

513
00:30:26,302 --> 00:30:28,862
What do you hand over,
and who builds the image?

514
00:30:29,100 --> 00:30:33,405
Two: which two settings make the meter run
while nobody uses the app,

515
00:30:33,405 --> 00:30:35,713
and what does a minimum instance buy?

516
00:30:35,950 --> 00:30:38,446
Three: who may call it, and who it runs as.

517
00:30:38,766 --> 00:30:42,307
Which setting answers each,
and why is no key file needed?

518
00:30:42,550 --> 00:30:46,794
Four: why does the job set a command
and eleven separate arguments,

519
00:30:46,794 --> 00:30:48,694
and what happens with neither?

520
00:30:48,933 --> 00:30:52,211
Five: name the lecture's three downsides
of serverless,

521
00:30:52,211 --> 00:30:54,714
and what this tutorial gives you for each.

